          "summary": "Agentic AI systems can improve by searching longer, receiving additional support, or modifying how they propose and verify outputs.\nA performance score does not distinguish these mechanisms. 绩效分数无法区分这些机制。\nWe compare these changes through bounded verification with hidden terminal randomness. 我们通过有界验证将这些变化与隐藏的终端随机性进行比较。\nA stage specifies admissible transcripts, polynomial bounds, an alternating verification protocol, and a terminal checker. 一个阶段指定可接受的成绩单、多项式界限、交替验证协议和终端检查器。\nIts native reach uses default support; its closure frontier permits all support already admitted by the interface. 其原生覆盖范围使用默认支持；其闭包边界允许接口已承认的所有支持。\nUnder a uniform pointwise probability gap and task-relative soundness, these are well-defined languages.\nWe prove that independent majority amplification preserves both languages, whereas existential acceptance over random tapes can admit incorrect outputs. 我们证明了独立多数放大保留了这两种语言，而随机磁带的存在接受可以承认错误的输出。\nExact verification is the zero-randomness case, with placement and completeness results.\nThe randomized-verifier classes satisfy $\\Sigma_k^{\\mathrm{P}}\\subseteq\\Sigma_k^{\\mathrm{RV}}\\subseteq\\Sigma_{k+1}^{\\mathrm{P}}$; strict enlargement and depth separation require explicit complexity assumptions, while $\\mathrm{BPP}=\\mathrm{P}$ yields exact companions with the same frontiers.\nRepresentation analysis separates invariant acceptance from core-versus-support labels that can change under refactoring. 表示分析将不变接受与可以在重构下更改的核心与支持标签分开。\nFor recursive self-improvement, uniformly bounded self-modification under a common sound interpreter and fixed verification protocol remains within the same verification class.\nA separate conditional-error budget controls false selection across adaptively chosen candidates. 单独的条件错误预算控制对自适应选择的候选人的错误选择。\nA quota-enforced XOR-synthesis family separates unbounded ratios of search success from changes in the accepted languages; exact and probabilistic audits check the resulting evidence requirements.\nThe framework ties self-improvement claims to obligations on correctness, admissible evidence, verification resources, and selection error.",
